<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wittenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lutherstadt Wittenberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wittenberg, officially Lutherstadt Wittenberg, is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was the seat of the Ascanian dukes of Saxe-Wittenberg and then of the Wettin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-wittenberg">University of Wittenberg</a> was founded in 1502. There <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> published his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a> in 1517, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Its Luther memorials are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wittenberg.jpg" alt="Wittenberg" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Toniklemm · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stadtkirche_Marktplatz_Schlosskirche_Wittenberg_Luftbild_2024_Toni_Klemm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wittenberg lies on the River Elbe, 60 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and 90 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Historical documents first mention it in 1180 as a small village founded by Flemish colonists under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a>. In 1260 it became the residence of the dukes of Saxe-Wittenberg, and in 1293 it received a town charter. Its central position made it an important trade centre over the following centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the local Ascanian line died out in 1422, Saxe-Wittenberg passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a>. Frederick III, called the Wise, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> from 1486 to 1525, made it his residence, and the town became an important political and cultural centre. The Castle Church was built between 1496 and 1506. Frederick founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-wittenberg">University of Wittenberg</a> in 1502. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> was professor of theology there from 1508 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philipp Melanchthon</a> professor of Greek from 1518. On 31 October 1517 Luther is said to have nailed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a> on indulgences to the door of the Castle Church, an event taken as the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</p><p>The Zwickau prophets, an early Anabaptist group, arrived in late 1521 and were suppressed by Luther when he returned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> in spring 1522. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulation-of-wittenberg">Capitulation of Wittenberg</a> in 1547, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-frederick-i-elector-of-saxony">John Frederick the Magnanimous</a> gave up the electoral dignity and most of his lands to the Albertine branch of the Wettins. The town was bombarded in 1760 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Wittenberg has the title Lutherstadt because of its ties to Luther and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. Its old town keeps the Castle Church, whose present bronze doors of 1858 bear the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text of the theses, and holds the tombs of Luther, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Melanchthon</a> and the electors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-elector-of-saxony">Frederick the Wise</a> and John the Constant. St. Mary's Church, where Luther often preached, has paintings by Lucas Cranach the Elder, who ran a print shop in the town. The Lutherhaus and Melanchthon's house are museums. The buildings associated with Luther and Melanchthon were added with other sites at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisleben">Eisleben</a> to the UNESCO World Heritage list in 1996. The town had 46,008 inhabitants in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wittenberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Martin Luther himself urged Albert, at <b>Wittenberg</b> in 1522 and at Nuremberg in 1524, to turn the Teutonic Order's land into a secular principality, and helped broker the deal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The name Saxony itself travelled: carried by the electoral title of Saxe-<b>Wittenberg</b> to the Wettin lands on the upper Elbe, it now names a German state far from the original duchy, whose heartland became Lower Saxony and Westphalia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After the old Duchy of Saxony was broken up in 1180, the name and ducal rank passed to the Ascanian dukes, whose line divided into Saxe-Lauenburg and Saxe-<b>Wittenberg</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>This fragmentation allowed the Reformation, launched by Martin Luther at <b>Wittenberg</b> in 1517, to survive where a centralised monarchy might have crushed it, and it encouraged intellectual competition among rival courts and universities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dominican friar Johann Tetzel preached it near the borders of Saxony in 1517, and people from <b>Wittenberg</b> crossed the border to buy.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>It is believed to have appeared in Joseph Klug's <b>Wittenberg</b> hymnal of 1529 under the title of the 46th Psalm, and before that in Hans Weiss's Wittenberg hymnal of 1528, but no copy of either remains.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-mighty-fortress-is-our-god">A Mighty Fortress Is Our God</a></li></ul></section>
